  "protocol_version": "v3",
  "resisted": "Nothing structural: no hidden node needed splitting (13 input splits, 3 consolidations). Node 14653 (area code 408) is never active on train (dead); 252 (state:44 / very low international charge) is active on 100 rows with at most +0.03 logit. The gate under-predicts churn for frequent callers with low day usage (CSC>=4 and day minutes <160: 113 rows, model 0.52 vs empirical 0.88). The voicemail chain is exactly linear, and mean-ablating it raises train AUC (0.8944 -> 0.8979): its extra voicemail-plan penalty slightly hurts ranking. closed_form_max_abs_err 0.043 (state_offsets) is 3-significant-figure rounding on state one-hots (standardised values up to 8); the numeric function is exact (2.7e-15).",
  "tool_bugs": "None new. Following the sibling agent's note, all splits were input splits before consolidation (no hidden split was needed). The Bash sandbox refused shell loops/variables/heredocs around uv, so tool calls were driven from a scratch Python script that runs experiments/species_leads/xn.py per call (all calls logged).",
  "summary": "logit P(churn) = -1.15 + a sparse linear backbone over 33 inputs (day minutes +1.84 per SD, voicemail plan -0.87, international plan +0.76, international charge +0.69, voicemail messages +0.58, evening minutes +0.50, ...) + a service-call x low-day-usage gate + a 4+-calls hinge + exactly linear voicemail, state-offset and area-code units, all additive in the logit. The gate is a low-day-minutes hinge whose kink moves up with each customer-service call (median 147 day minutes at 0 calls, 215 at 4, 270 at 6), lowered by high evening charge and switched off in state:0, so with the direct day-minutes edge the logit is V-shaped in day minutes and frequent callers with low usage churn. Service calls act only through hinges. Train AUC 0.894, accuracy 0.896.",
